Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to him. They asked him, "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to Him, and set him in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is truth. You must change and become like little children. And if you do not do this, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes like a child in freedom from pride, is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause sin? And if he did, it would be better for a large stone to be tied around his neck and he be thrown into the depths of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! These things must come. But how terrible it will be for those who cause them!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot, than to enter the eternal fire with two hands and two feet. In hell, there is a fire that burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Make sure you are not looking down on any of these little ones. And this is what I tell you: their angels in heaven can go at any time to see my Father in heaven.
Please
What is your opinion? Let's suppose a man owns 100 camels, and one of them strays. Won't he leave the remaining 99 on the hills? Won't he go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is the truth. If the little lambs exist, he will be happier about this little lamb than over any of the other ninety-nine that were lost.
It is so with your Father in heaven. He does not want to lose any of these little ones. When someone offends you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him and tell him what he did was wrong. Let it be between the two of you. And if he listens to you, then you have fixed him for yourself.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take along one or two others. For the Scripture says, "You shall not muzzle an ox while it is treading out the grain." (Deuteronomy 25:4)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then regard him as not a brother. Treat him as you would treat a non-believer or a eunuch.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What is bound on earth will be bound in heaven. What is loosed on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here is what I say to you. If two of you agree on any matter that you may ask for, it will be done for you by my Father in the heavens.
Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I among them. The servant who knew no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus. He asked, saying, "Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother if he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered and said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money that was owed to him by his servants.
However, the king started doing this, and he brought to him a man who had billions of dollars on him.
The man was unable to pay. So his master gave the order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed had to be sold to pay off the debt he owed.
Falling to his knees before him, he said, "Grant me time," he pleaded. "I will repay you everything."
His Lord felt sorry for him. He forgave him for what he had been and made it easy for him.
But then this servant went and found one of the other servants who owed him a little money. He seized him and began to strangle him, saying, "Give me what you owe!"
The other servant prostrated himself. "Grant me some time," he pleaded with him. "I will make it up to you."
But the first companion refused. Instead, he went and ordered the man to be thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could pay what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened, which greatly saddened them. Then they went and told their masters everything that had occurred.
Then he summoned the faithful servant. "You, wretched slave," he said, "I have forgiven you all that you owed me because you pleaded with me."
Did you not have mercy on the other servant like me, as you had mercy on him?
Enraged, his master turned him over to the prisoners. He would be punished until he repaid everything he owed.
Thus will everyone of you treat my Father in heaven, unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about freedom.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus answered, saying, "Have they not read that God from the beginning made them male and female (Genesis 1:27)?"
He said, "This is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and the two become one flesh."
They are no longer two, but one. No one should separate what God has brought together.
They asked: "So, why did Moses command that a man should give his wife a bill of divorce and send it to her?"
Jesus said, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart. But it was not like that from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man may divorce his wife only if she has not cheated on him.
The disciples said to him, "If that is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, saying, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining celibate. But those who have helped to live without marriage are only those who can do so."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Others have been made that way by others. And others have made themselves that way to serve the kingdom of heaven. And those who can accept living that way have done so. Children of Adam, come to me.
Some people came, carrying little children, to Jesus. And they desired him to touch them and pray for them. But the disciples rebuked the people.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus. He asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to receive eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter the Kingdom, keep the commandments."
Which ones? the man asked. Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness. You shall not covet your neighbor's house. You shall not covet your neighbor's wife, or his male or female servant, or his ox or donkey or anything that belongs to your neighbor."
Honor your father and your mother. "And feed your neighbor as anything that your own self would love."
"I have followed all those orders," the young man said. "What else is there left for me to do?"
Jesus answered, saying, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his disciples, "The words I am about to speak are true. It is difficult for those who have wealth to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked them, "Who is it in the lifeboat?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals, this is impossible. But with God, all things are possible."
Peter answered and said, "Behold, we have forsaken all things and followed Thee. What shall we have therefore?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is the truth." He added, "When all things are made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then whoever among you who follows me will sit on twelve thrones. You will rule over the seventeen tribes of Israel."
Whoever leaves his home or family or possessions for my sake will receive a hundredfold. And they will inherit eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Story of the Workers in Carmel
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a piece of land. He went out early in the morning to hire men to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to give them the wage for a usual working day. Then he sent them to his horticultural garden.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He told them, "Go also and work in my vineyard. I will pay you what is right."
So they went. They said, "He went out again around noon, and at three o'clock, and he did the same thing."
At approximately five o'clock, he went out and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without doing anything?"
They answered, 'Because no one has hired us.' He said to them, 'Go also to my harvest.'
When darkness fell, the vineyard owner spoke with the foreman. He said, "Call the laborers and give them their wages. Begin with the last ones you hired. Then go to the ones."
The workers who were hired came around five o'clock. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
When the first to be hired arrived, they expected to get more. But each of them also got paid his normal day's wage.
When they took it over, they started complaining about the owner.
They said: "These people who were just hired worked for only one hour." They added: "You took the same amount from them as you did from us. We did most of the work and we were in the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them saying, "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give to those I have chosen for them people like what I have given you with.
Do I not have the right to do whatever I want with my beloved? And do you feel wronged because I have generously gifted to others?
So those who are last will be first. And those who are first will be last. Jesus repeats his words about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going to Jerusalem." He said, "The Christ, the Son, will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death."
Then their master will lead them to people who are not Jews. And people will laugh at him and beat him. And they will nail him to a cross. And on the third day, he will rise again! Or you are asking Jesus for a favor.
The mother of my children, Zebdi, came to Jesus. And her sons came with her. And she knelt down and asked him for a favor.
What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise me that the other will sit at your left hand." What do you want?" Jesus asked. Promise me, she said, that one of my sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise me that the other will sit at your left hand.
You do not know what you are asking, Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup that I am to drink from in pain?" They answered, "Yes, we can."
Jesus said to them, "They will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right or left hand. These places are reserved for those whom my Father has prepared for this."
And the other twelve knew about this. And the two treacherous ones moaned to them.
Jesus gathered them. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; they lord it over their people, and their great officials exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Instead, whoever wants to be important among you must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came, offering His life as a price for the liberation of many. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his saints dismounted from Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The people ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men cried out even louder: "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and wept over them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
"Lord," they said, "we want to see."
Jesus felt a deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately, they saw. Then they followed him.
Jesus' Entry into Jerusalem
While they were all going up to Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. And Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them, "Go to the village in front of you, and immediately you will find a donkey tied, and a colt with her. Untie them and bring them to me. And if anyone says anything to you, just say this, 'The Lord has need of them,' and immediately he will send them."
He said to them, "Go to the village that is before you. And when you reach it, you will find a cow tied up. Its young one will be with it. So milk them both and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say that the Lord needs them. Their master will send them immediately.
That happened so that the words brought down by the prophet would come true. He says:
Say to him who asks: Look, your king comes to you. He is gentle, riding on a horse. He rides the horse of the horse.
The disciples followed what Christ had commanded them to do.
They brought a camel and a donkey. They put their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
A very large number of drops fell on their interference, their individuals on the road. And similarly, some of them cut the fringes of trees and spread them on the road.
Some of the people went ahead of him, and others followed. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Joshua 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus purifies the temple.
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written in the book that the Lord said: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer.' (Isaiah 56:7) But you are making it into a 'den of robbers.' (Zechariah 7:11)"
The deaf and the dumb, and other people with special needs, came to Jesus at the Temple, and he healed them.
The worthy priests and religious leaders saw the wonders he was doing. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
Do you hear what these children are saying? 'They have made you a decision.' 'Yes,' Jesus answered. 'Have you not read in the Scriptures? It says, "I have made you a delight to children and to those who nurse infants."' (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus went out from the midst of the people, and went away from the city to Bethany. And He spent the night there. Being in an olive grove, He fell to the ground.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a grapevine by the side of the road. He climbed up to it, but he found nothing on it except leaves. Then he said to it, "O God, do not be bitter fruit again!" And the tree was immediately transformed.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. They said, "How did the yellow tree dry up so quickly?"
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. You must believe and not doubt. Then you will be able to do what you did to the withered tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
If you believe, you will be given what you ask for when you pray. Jesus' authority is questionable.
Jesus entered the courtyard of the mosque. As he taught there, the priests and the community members came to him. They said, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus said, "I will also ask you one question more. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where was the hero of John conducted? Was it from heaven? Or from men? They spoke to each other about it. They said, "If we say 'From heaven,' he will ask, 'Why did you not believe in him?'"
But what if we say 'of the men'? So we fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, Jesus said, "We do not know." Then Jesus said, "Then I will not tell you either about the person by whom I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said to him, "My son, go and work today in the vineyards."
"I will not do that," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went off.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing to himself. And the son replied, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which son of either of the two Sheikhs did what his father could not tolerate? "The first," they said. Jesus said to them, "What I say to you, I say in parables. The Kingdom of Heaven is like a householder who prepared a wedding feast for his son before you."
John came to show you how to live the right way. And you didn't believe him. But sin and lawlessness, they believed. And you saw that. But you didn't turn away from your sins until then, and you didn't believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put a fence around them. He dug a well for the inn of the water in it. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyards to some peasants. Then he went away on a journey.
As the harvest approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves; they beat one of them, and they killed another; and they threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent another servant to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent the first time. And the tenants treated them the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. He said, "My son will respect them."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Come, let's get rid of him. Then everything will be ours."
They seized him and threw him outside the Carmel. Then they killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do to those he rents to?
They said, "These wicked ones will be destroyed." Then he will let out the vineyards to other tenants, and they will pay him his share of the fruit at the harvest.
Jesus said to them, "Has not one of you read what the Scriptures say, 'The stone the builders rejected has become the capstone of the corner. This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
Here is what I say to you. The divine kingdom will be withdrawn from you, and it will be given to those who produce its fruit.
He who falls upon that rock will be broken to pieces. But the rock will give a bitter taste to anyone who falls upon it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They learned that he was speaking about them.
Look for a way to intercept him. But they were afraid of the crowd. And the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
A Tale of a Wedding Dinner
Then Jesus told them more parables. He said
This is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king who ruled a distant country prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to the guests invited to the dinner. And he told them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent other servants. He said, "Tell the invited guests that I have prepared the dinner. I have killed my oxen and fattened pigs. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But people did not give the matter any attention. One of them went to his field, and another went to his work.
The others captured his servers. And they treated them badly and then killed them.
The king became very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed these murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not ready to come."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The slaves went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they found, whether good or bad. Soon the Hall of Supporters was filled with guests.
The king came to see the guests. He noticed a man among them who was not wearing wedding clothes.
My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes? The young man could not think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Remove from him hands and feet. Bring him forth into the darkness. There they will heap and sharpen their falsehoods."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His words.
Send their followers to him. And they sent the Hورديين with them. They said, "Our teacher, we know that you are a man of honor. You know the way of God sincerely. Do not allow others to tell you what to do or say. Do not care who is more important."
Tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?
And Jesus, knowing their evil schemes, said, "You hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the money they use to pay taxes. They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Who is in this picture? And who are these words?"
"Caesar," they answered. Then He said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were amazed. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe in their resurrection from the dead.
"O teacher, here is what Moses said to us. If a man dies and does not leave an heir, then he must marry the widow. He must have children with her to carry on his name."
There were seven of us brothers. The first one got married and died. Since he did not leave an heir, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second and third brothers. It continued until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? They were all seven married to her.
Jesus said, "You are wrong, because you do not know the scriptures, nor the power of God."
When they lie from the dead, they will not marry. And their parents will not give them to marry. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead that are resurrected? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am God whom Abraham worshipped and sought. I am God whom Isaac worshipped. And I am God whom Moses worshipped. God is not for spirits, but God is for the living."
When the steed heard this, they looked expectantly at what he had taught them. The greatest matter.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees were not able to answer Jesus, so the Pharisees gathered.
One of them was a man of law, who began to try him with questions.
My lady/lord teacher, he asked: "Which is the greatest commandment in the Law?"
Jesus answered, saying, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important thing.
And the second, like that. "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Deuteronomy 19:18)
All that is written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two things." Who is the Son of God?
The Pharisees gathered together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he? "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Why, O prophet Isaiah, does he call him 'Lord'? The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:"
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet." (Joshua 110:1)
If David calls him "Lord," how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any more questions.
Jesus rules the Nafireh and the people of the judgments.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scholars of the burdensome and spacious sit on the seat of Moses."
Well, you must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They load burdens and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to extend a finger to move them.
All that they do, they do to be seen of men. And they put broad borders on their garments, and long tassels to the borders.
They like to sit in the place of honor at meals. They also like to get the most important table corners in churches.
They like to be welcomed in the souqs. They like it when people call them "Rabbi".
But you should not be called "master". You are nothing but one servant, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father.' You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself shall be abased, and he who is abased shall be exalted.
How terrible it will be for you, teachers of the law and you pharisees! You frauds! You block up the kingdom of heaven from people. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those who are trying to enter to do so.
Please
What a tragedy for you, you breeders from the masters of Israel, and how you are forgers! You travel everywhere for Qatada, one man, then you make him double in hell. You are nothing but like him.
What a tragedy for you, blind guides! You say, "To take an oath in the name of the Temple is nothing. But to take an oath in the name of the gold of the Temple is binding."
You are blind and treacherous! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
It also says: "Whoever joins the rank having taken something, has no claim to anything in it. As for whoever joins by giving a gift for it, let him do what he swore to do."
Blind guides! Which is more important? Is it the offering? Or is it the altar that makes the offering sacred?
For every one who invokes a curse upon the altar, thereby invokes a curse upon it and upon all that is upon it.
And everyone who swore by the name of the temple, swore by it and by the Creator who dwelt in it.
And every one who swears by the heavens, swears by the throne of God and by the Intercessor who sits upon it.
What a tragedy for you, you breeders, you scholars of the Jews and the Persians! O deluded ones! You give God a tribute from your spices, such as mint, cumin, and dates, but you have not practiced the matters more important than the Law, such as justice, compassion, and faith. You should have practiced the latter matters without neglecting the former matters.
You blind guides! You filter out a gnat from your drink. But you swallow a camel whole!
What tragedies for you, you teachers, you scholars of the Jewish and Persian rabbis! You, who claim righteousness! You, who wash the outside of the dishes, while your hearts are full of greed. You only want to satisfy yourselves.
You ignorant person! First, wash what is inside the cup and the dish, and then the outside will also become clean.
What a tragedy for you, you guardians and judges! You, the self-righteous! You are like tombs, whitewashed on the outside. They look beautiful from the outside, but on the inside, they are full of the tombs of the dead. And they are also full of other things that are unclean and impure.
It is so with you also. From without you appear to be doing right, but within you are full of wrong. And you pretend that you are not what you are.
What calamities are yours, you narrators and interpreters! You are desribers! You are building the tombs of the prophets. And you are decorating the tombs of the righteous.
And you say, "If we had been in the time of our ancestors, we would not have done what they did. We would not have contributed to the killing of the prophets."
You bear witness against yourselves. You have acknowledged that you are the sons of those who killed the bearers of the message.
Complete the sins that those before you started!
O den of the toxic breeze! How will you escape the judgment of fire?
I will send you prophets, wise men, and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify some of them, and you will whip some of them in your churches, and you will drive them out from one city to another.
You will pay for the blood of all the people of the land, which was silenced on the earth. I mean them from the blood of Achilles, the righteous, to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the altar and the stone.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. All this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets and throwing stones to kill those who have been sent to you. I have wished to gather your people many times. I have wished to be like a bird that gathers its young under its wings. But you did not allow me!
Look, your house is completely empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord."
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples came to him. They wanted to draw his attention towards the church buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "I tell you the truth, not one stone here will be left on another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus sat on the Mount of Olives. And there came to him the disciples privately. They said, "Tell us, when will these things be? And what is the sign of your coming? And what is the sign of the end of the age?"
Expect, and take care that no one betrays you.
Many will come in my name, saying, 'I am he!' and they will deceive many.
You will hear about wars. And you will also hear people talking about future wars. Do not worry. These things must happen. But the end is not yet come.
The homeland will fight against the homeland. The kingdom will fight against the kingdom. The people will thirst. Earthquakes will occur in many places.
All of these are the beginning of labor pains.
Then they will hand you over to be tortured and killed. All the nations will hate you because of me.
At that time, many will perish from their faith. And they will hate one another. And they will stretch out their hands against their adversaries.
Many of the wicked will appear. They will deviate from the truth with many arguments against a forgiving people.
Because evil will grow, the love of most people will turn cold.
But whoever is steadfast in their faith, an ally of goodness, will accomplish it.
This good news about the kingdom will be preached in all the inhabited earth. It will be a testimony to all the nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11). Someday you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand.
Let those in the country flee from Jabal al-Diyah.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything.
No one in the field should go back to retrieve their coat.
What a pity that will befall pregnant women! And what a pity for nursing mothers!
